# The Mathworld Classification and Directedness

While doing research for the labyrinth, I came across a set of interesting articles from [Wolfram Mathworld](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Polyomino.html). If we look up the topic ["Lattice Polygons"](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/topics/LatticePolygons.html), we come across a collection of curious special cases of polyominoes:

- [Convex Polyomino](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/ConvexPolyomino.html)
- [Column-](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Column-ConvexPolyomino.html) and [Row-Convex Polyomino](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Row-ConvexPolyomino.html)
- [Directed Convex Polyomino](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/DirectedConvexPolyomino.html)
- [Stack Polyomino](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/StackPolyomino.html)
- [Staircase Polygon](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/StaircasePolygon.html)
- [Bar Graph Polygon](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/BarGraphPolygon.html)
- [Ferrers Graph Polygon](https://mathworld.wolfram.com/FerrersGraphPolygon.html)

These articles seem to specify different groups of polyominoes, so maybe that's what we're looking for? We could just assign a different color to each type of polyomino mentioned in one of these articles and call it a day. But I'm not satisfied with just an arbitrary categorization without understanding what it means. I had a couple of questions:

<!-- TODO I feel like I should go into more detail; either put definitions in there or something. -->

1. Why _these_ classes? What makes them mathematically interesting?
2. How are these classes related to each other?
3. Is this categorization complete? What if someone discovers (or defines) a new class? Would we need to amend our list or move stuff around?

For the first question, we have to go back to one of the fundamental things that makes polyominoes fascinating combinatorial objects: _we don't have a formula for how to calculate them_. Restricting polyominoes to certain classes allows us to find a kind of formula (more specifically: [generating function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generating_function)) for counting the number of polyominoes, which might help us solve the bigger question of how many polyominoes there are. Indeed, most of these articles, and the [paper](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0012365X9500003F) they come from, list the (obscenely complicated) generating functions of the classes they describe.

For the second question, yes! These classes form a hierarchy, just like with symmetry and tiling:

<HierarchyChart nodes={hierarchy1Nodes} edges={hierarchy1Edges} />

It's not too hard to show that, for example, a mino that is both a staircase and a stack necessarily has to be a Ferrers diagram polyomino.

The third condition is a little bit harder. After all, we could just add one of our symmetry classes to this list and that's a new color we can add. We want to some way to say that all these classes are related somehow. That they are the only possible categorizations based on some property of polyominoes, like with symmetry. And to do that, we need to look deeper at the definitions of these classes.

### Convexity and Directedness

A couple of words keep coming up in these definitions: _convex_ and _directed_.

A convex polyomino is easy to intuit: it's a polyomino where there aren't any "horseshoe" segments, or concavities. Formally, you can draw a line through any column or row and it will not cut through two different segments of the mino. We can use "row-convex" or "column-convex" (or generally, "semi-convex") to denote a polyomino that you can draw that line in one direction, or another.

(TODO: example of each kind)

"Directed" is tougher to define. It means that every cell of the polyomino can be reached by going right or up from a starting square. Intuitively, it means that if you hold the mino up diagonally and drop a marble in it, it can conceivably reach every square without going against gravity.

(TODO: definition of directed and marble dropping example)

If we want to specify directions (say, $v$, $h$), we can say a polyomino is $vh$-directed.

We can use these two definitions to categorize all the classes. A staircase is a convex polyomino that is directed from two opposite corners. A stack is a polyomino that is directed from two adjacent corners, and so on. We can use directedness and semi-convexity to refine our hierarchy from before:

<HierarchyChart nodes={hierarchy2Nodes} edges={hierarchy2Edges} />

We can reasonably stop here and be satisfied with our classification. In fact, for a while this was the hierarchy that I used for the Labyrinth. But something about this still felt slapdash to me. Why are _convexity_ and _directedness_ the two properties we care about? Why not combine symmetry and convexity, or graphs and directedness? Is there something about these two properties that makes it easier to count polyominoes of that type?

## Orthogonal directedness

One of my favorite aspects of math is to take a concept and loosen it, ease up its restrictions, and see what mathematics comes out. This is how the natural numbers become integers, how groups become categories, how metrics become topologies. We can do this with the notion of "directedness" as well. Instead of reaching any square by going right and up, what if we consider minos where you can reach any square by going left, right, or up? In our intuitive analogy, instead of hanging our mino by a corner we keep it loose and let a marble roll down:

(TODO: image of a marble run of a mino on its side)

Let's call our old form of directedness _diagonally directed_ and define this new one as _orthogonally directed_:

**Definition** A polyomino is _orthogonally directed_ if every cell can be reached from a root cell by going only three directions (say, left, right and up). A polyomino is $d$-directed in a direction $d$ if it is orthogonally directed (it never goes in the opposite direction of $d$).

<MinoDiv mino={Polyomino.fromString("011_101_111_101")} size={16} />

There are some differences between diagonal and orthogonal directedness. A diagonally directed mino must always have a single root cell, in the corner of its bounding box, while an orthogonally directed mino can have a straight line of cells on its side. Intuitively, a polyomino is `u`-directed if it doesn't have any downward facing concavities: it doesn't contain anything that looks like <MinoDiv mino={uPentomino} size={12} />.

Just like we defined classes based on different corners that a mino is diagonally directed from, we can ask minos be orthogonally directed from different sides and see what they look like:

(TODO: some examples of minos directed from multiple sides)

It seems like, when a mino doesn't have holes, a semi-convex mino is one that's side-directed from two opposite sides!

**Theorem** A polyomino is semi-convex if and only if it is [punctureless](/glossary/punctureless) and orthogonally directed from two opposite sides.

_Proof_ Assume a polyomino is row-convex. Then every row is a contiguous set of cells. The mino cannot have a puncture, since that would break up the row that the hole is in. We proceed by induction. Every cell in the bottom row can be reached by a series of `l` and `r` moves, since the row is contiguous. Assume all rows below can be reached, and consider a cell $c$ in a row. Ther is a cell $c'$ in the same row that can be reached from a cell $c''$ row below by using a `u` move. By the inductive hypothesis, $c''$ can be reached using `l`, `r`, and `u` moves, and so $c$ can be reached by going `u` to $c'$ then either `l` or `r`. A similar argument by replacing "below" with "above", and `u` with `d` shows that it is `d` directed as well, and rotating everything by 90deg will work for column-convex minos.

For the converse, Let $M$ be a mino that is is `u`-directed and `d`-directed and does not contain punctures. Assume for the sake of contradiction that $M$ is not row-convex. That is, there are cells $c_1$ and $c_2$ in the same row of $M$ that are separated by a hole cell $h \notin M$. By definition of directedness, there is a cell $c_d$ on the top row of the mino that is connected by a path to $c_1$ and $c_2$, and similarly a cell $c_u$ on the bottom row. Then the path that goes through $c_u$, $c_1$, $c_d$, $c_2$ forms a loop that surrounds $h$, which means that $h$ is a puncture, which is a contradiction.

**Corollary** A polyomino is convex iff it is punctureless and orthogonally directed from all four sides.

Note that we specify _punctureless_: that is, a mino with no internal holes. For the purposes of the labyrinth, we can mostly set aside punctured polyominoes, since the only punctured octomino is the "O" octomino:

<MinoDiv mino={Polyomino.fromString("111_101_111")} size={16} />

But for now, we can replace our definitions on convexity with orthogonal directedness. We can use the notation $\text{Dir}_{o}^{d}$ where $o$ denotes the number of sides a mino is orthogonally directed from, and $d$ denotes the number of corners. When $o$ or $d = 2$, we have to specify whether the two sides/corners are opposite or adjacent, so we specify $c$ (for cis-) for two adjacent sides/corners or $t$ (for trans-) for opposite sides/corners. We now have a hierarchy that relies only only on one concept ("how many directions do you need to reach every cell").

<HierarchyChart nodes={hierarchy3Nodes} edges={hierarchy3Edges} />

Note that if a mino is corner-directed, it has to be orthogonally-directed in the two sides that adjacent to that corner. Specifically:

**Theorem** If a polyomino is $vh$-directed, then it is $v$-directed and $h$-directed.

Now that we have a finer-grained definition, we have more classes than before! In particular, we have some additional classes that the definitions of semi-convexity weren't granular enough to encode: ["crescents"](/classes/crescent), which side-directed in three out of four sides, and ["bent trees"](/classes/bent-tree), which are side-directed from three sides.

But is this all? Can we extend the definition of directedness even further? Well, diagonal directedness is when you can travel in two directions, and orthogonal directedness is when you can travel in three. If we're only allowed to travel in one direction, all we can make is a straight line polyomino, which doesn't seem interesting enough to get its own class. If we can travel in four directions... then we aren't restricted at all. We can be assured this hierarchy captures everything interesting related to this "directedness" property.

I'm quite proud of this hierarchy. As far as I know, I'm the only one who has extended this notion of directedness to "orthogonal directedness" and found the relation to semi-convex minos. I think it's an elegant set up that's similar to the symmetry hierarchy.

However, I'm still not _quite_ satisfied with it. With the current labels, this is only the classification for _punctureless_ polyominoes. There's still a second concept of "punctures" that we haven't dealt with, and a second hierarchy parallel to the one above but with the "punctureless" condition removed. We will have "punctured rectangles", "punctured staircases", "punctured convex" (which feels oxymoronic). In a way, we're still in the same place as before: instead of "directedness" and "semi-convexity" as two unrelated building blocks, we have "directedness" and "punctures" as two unrelated building blocks. We could just say we don't care whether a mino has a puncture or not, but "convexity" seems like a really useful concept to keep: there's a lot of things we can say about convex minos that we can't about "punctured convex" minos. In the next section, we will see that punctures relate in a natural way to directedness, but to do so we will need to take a little detour to talk about _boundaries_.

